PROTEIN-PROTEIN INTERACTION (PPI) NETWORK ANALYSIS – TP53

Exploring the functional interaction network of the human TP53 tumor-suppressor protein using the STRING database.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
1. METHODOLOGY & DATABASE CONFIGURATION

To understand the cellular context and functional interactions of the tumor-suppressor protein TP53, an interaction network was generated using the STRING database (Search Tool for the Retrieval of Interacting Genes/Proteins).

Analysis Setup

• Target Protein: TP53 (Cellular tumor antigen p53)
• Organism: Homo sapiens (Human)
• Database: STRING
• Interaction Sources: Experimental evidence, curated databases, text mining, co-expression, and genomic context
• Confidence Score: Medium confidence (≥ 0.400)
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2. NETWORK OBSERVATIONS & ANALYSIS

The resulting STRING network shows a cluster of proteins functionally associated with TP53, highlighting its role as an important regulatory hub involved in cellular stress and DNA-damage responses.

Q1. How many proteins interact with TP53?

Using the selected STRING network configuration, the network contains 11 total nodes, consisting of TP53 and 10 associated proteins.
These proteins represent functional associations identified by STRING based on the selected evidence sources and confidence threshold.

Q2. Which proteins appear most closely connected?

Based on the network visualization and interaction evidence, several proteins show strong associations with TP53, including:
• MDM2
• ATM
• CHEK2
• EP300
• BRCA1
Among these, MDM2 shows a particularly strong association with TP53 in the analyzed network.

Q3. Which interactions have the highest confidence?

The TP53–MDM2 and TP53–ATM interactions show very high confidence scores in the analyzed network.
Note: STRING confidence scores represent the strength of the available evidence supporting an interaction or functional association. They should not be interpreted directly as binding strength.
